<div>
  <p>If a page on another site offers your product without your permission, you can ask us to send the site a takedown notice. We check the page and write the notice. You read it and sign it, and we send it for you. Nothing goes out until you sign.</p>
  <p>This article is not legal advice.</p>
  <p><strong>In this article:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#before-you-start" target="_self">Before you start</a></li>
    <li><a href="#report-a-page" target="_self">Report a page</a></li>
    <li><a href="#what-each-status-means" target="_self">What each status means</a></li>
    <li><a href="#what-you-sign" target="_self">What you sign</a></li>
    <li><a href="#if-someone-disputes-your-notice" target="_self">If someone disputes your notice</a></li>
    <li><a href="#how-long-it-takes" target="_self">How long it takes</a></li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h3>
  <p>Only the account owner and team members with the admin role can report piracy, because the notice has your legal name in it. You can report a product when all of these are true:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You have confirmed your email, completed a payout, and reached $100 in sales.</li>
    <li>Your payout information has your legal name.</li>
    <li>The product is yours, is published, has no collaborator, and has at least one sale.</li>
    <li>Your account is not suspended.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You can file 5 reports each calendar month. Each report covers one page and one product.</p>
  <p>To report a page on Gumroad, see <a href="286-how-do-i-report-a-gumroad-creator">Report a Gumroad creator</a> instead.</p>
  <h3 id="report-a-page">Report a page</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>Products</strong>, open the menu on the product, and choose <strong>Report piracy</strong>. If you do not see this option, the feature is not turned on for your account.</li>
    <li>Paste the link to the page that offers your product, and submit. If something is missing, the form tells you what to fix.</li>
    <li>We check the page. When the notice is ready, we email you.</li>
    <li>Read the notice, confirm each statement, type your full legal name, and click <strong>Sign notice</strong>.</li>
    <li>We send the notice to the site, with you in CC, and we email you when it is sent.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>To see your reports, go to <strong>Products</strong> and open the <strong>Piracy reports</strong> tab.</p>
  <h3 id="what-each-status-means">What each status means</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Checking the page:</strong> we are checking that the page offers your work, and that it is not a licensed or other authorized use.</li>
    <li><strong>Ready for you to sign:</strong> the notice is ready. Read it and sign it.</li>
    <li><strong>Signed, sending soon:</strong> we send it within about 15 minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Sent, waiting for the site:</strong> we sent the notice to the site. You do not need to do anything.</li>
    <li><strong>Disputed:</strong> the person who posted the page sent a counter-notice. You must act. See <a href="#if-someone-disputes-your-notice" target="_self">If someone disputes your notice</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Page removed:</strong> the site took the page down.</li>
    <li><strong>No response from the site:</strong> the site did not answer the notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Page put back:</strong> the site put the page back after a counter-notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Not sent:</strong> we could not confirm that the page offers your work, so we sent nothing. You cannot report the same page again for that product. If you have more evidence, contact support.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdrawn</strong> or <strong>Closed:</strong> the report ended without a result from the site.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="what-you-sign">What you sign</h3>
  <p>The notice goes to the site's copyright agent, the person the site names to receive these notices. It has your legal name, your email, your product, and the link to the page. It also has the statements that U.S. copyright law requires, including one under penalty of perjury that you own the work or can act for the owner.</p>
  <p>Before you sign, know that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The site can share the notice, with your name and email, with the person who posted the page. The notice may also be published, for example on Lumen, a public database of takedown notices.</li>
    <li>If you knowingly report a page that does not infringe your work, you can be liable for damages, costs, and attorneys' fees (17 U.S.C. § 512(f)).</li>
    <li>A review, a commentary, or a short excerpt can be fair use. Do not report those.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When you sign, we add your typed name and the date to the notice. After that, the text cannot change.</p>
  <h3 id="if-someone-disputes-your-notice">If someone disputes your notice</h3>
  <p>The person who posted the page can send the site a counter-notice, which says they have the right to share the work. When we get one, we email you its full text, and the report shows <strong>Disputed</strong> with two dates.</p>
  <p>Under U.S. copyright law, the site can put the page back 10 to 14 business days after it got the counter-notice. To stop that:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>File a court action against the person who posted the page, before the first date on your report.</li>
    <li>Reply to our email to tell us.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>We do not file for you. If you do nothing, the site can put the page back. The dates count weekdays only, so treat them as approximate.</p>
  <h3 id="how-long-it-takes">How long it takes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Checking the page:</strong> usually less than a day. If a person on our team needs to look, it can take a few days. If we do not yet have a verified takedown contact for the site, we cannot say when the report will move.</li>
    <li><strong>Signing:</strong> there is no deadline. The report waits for you.</li>
    <li><strong>Sending:</strong> within about 15 minutes after you sign.</li>
    <li><strong>The site's answer:</strong> there is no set time. A report can stay on <strong>Sent, waiting for the site</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
